{
  "hypothesis": "A fault scarp is a signed step in detrended topography: slope grows downhill across its crest and collapses across its base. The fall-line directional second derivative |grad(det_elev_slope) . unit_grad(det_elev)|, gated by structure-tensor coherence of det_elev, isolates that crest/base pair, whereas isotropic slope-break magnitude (BREAKLINE) also fires on drainage roughness. Faults offsetting only alluvium or like lithologies carry such a step with no magnetic/gravity contrast, so the H19-5 worm skeleton (and parent R0 on it) misses them, while expert DEM mapping -- the competition label source -- traces exactly this polarity.",
  "mechanism": "At fixed S=37,654, DTI rises when expected T gains outweigh denominator growth. Kept skeleton dots are chosen by exact marginal expected-DTI greedy (alpha 0.2, beta 0.8, triangular R=3px) under w=0.30+0.70*S_pct on H19-5 support, so coverage-redundant parent dots are the donors. Far dots take the strongest coherent scarp-polarity ridges first (descending S, 2.8px spacing floor): the pre-build screen gave this surface the best withheld-catalogue canary AUC of any untried DEM statistic (0.628012 vs BREAKLINE 0.621604) at comparable off-catalogue SGMC enrichment (far-set PROXY 0.067670 vs 0.072325; docs/HYPOTHESES_20261024_SCARPPOL.md).",
  "named_non_fault_process_that_could_mimic_it": "Pluvial Lake Lahontan shoreline benches and wave-cut terraces, alluvial-fan segment boundaries, incised channel banks, and bedding-plane breaks on dipping strata all produce a signed crest/base slope pair along the fall line with no Quaternary surface rupture.",
